WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.190
Amateur terrorist attacks and soap made from
liposuction fat?

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.960
"Fight Club" can be a confusing ride — but
we're here to help.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.380
The first two rules of "Fight Club" are to
never talk about Fight Club, but the film

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.439
itself also has a rule: never reveal the Narrator's
name.

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:22.869
The film avoids mentioning the name of the
protagonist, who's only called "Narrator"

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:23.960
in the ending credits.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.330
The character stays nameless so that he can
represent anyone who has lost their sense

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:29.690
of self and individuality.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:34.620
"Fight Club" cleverly references his lack
of a name when he is simply addressed as "sir"

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.090
by members of the club.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.380
The film goes as far as to give him multiple
names as he attends various support groups

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.910
under aliases.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.040
Marla Singer even asks him about it when they
first meet,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.170
"It doesn't have your name.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.449
Who are you?

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:49.449
Cornelius?

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:50.449
Rupert?”

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:51.449
[honking]

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:53.570
“Any of these stupid names you give each
night?"

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.280
Meanwhile, the narrator frequently uses the
phrase "I am Jack's sense of..." to express

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.190
how he is feeling at any given time.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.870
This is a reference to articles that he reads
early in the film.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.680
"I am Jack's medulla oblongata.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.830
Without me, Jack cannot regulate his heart
rate, blood pressure, or breathing."

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.110
The text on the "Fight Club" DVD, as well
as its bonus features, refer to the character

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.430
as Jack, and the chapter list for the film
is called "Jack's Chapters."

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.070
The character is also referred to as Jack
in the official shooting screenplay, which

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.260
Norton confirmed in the DVD commentary of
the movie.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:31.259
You would never describe Marla's relationship
with the Narrator as "healthy."

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:35.370
Marla regularly expresses her frustration
with the Narrator's behavior towards her.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:39.070
"You are such a nutcase, I can't even begin
to keep up"

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.270
Even the Narrator doesn't know that he's in
a sexual relationship with her until he realizes

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.979
that he himself is Tyler Durden, with whom
Marla has been having sex.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:52.770
Before that realization, the Narrator doesn't
seem to like her as a person, and therefore

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.820
Tyler doesn't respect her.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.020
Viewers would wonder, among other things,
why Marla would continue to stay with the

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.980
Narrator with everything he's putting her
through.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.399
It's established that the chain-smoking Marla
has grown accustomed to leading a self-destructive

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:10.000
lifestyle, possibly to the point that she
views toxic relationships as normal.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.129
This might come from the character's low self-esteem,
which makes her drawn to the Narrator especially

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:19.690
after he intervened as she tried to end her
own life.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.290
After the Narrator meets Tyler Durden on the
plane, he's drawn to the mysterious soap salesman

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.260
who seems to be the complete opposite of himself.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.620
There are times before their initial encounter
when the Narrator may not have noticed Tyler's

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.630
presence, but audiences have.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.700
With the exception of the first time Tyler
is clearly seen on the escalator, he's only

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.120
briefly visible to eagle-eyed viewers in single-frame
flashes.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.190
Given that Tyler was likely controlling the
Narrator's body while he slept, these single

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:50.390
frame flashes could be remnants of his memories
while sleepwalking.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:55.121
The explosion at the Narrator's condo was
caused by Tyler, and it happened before they

00:02:55.121 --> 00:02:56.250
met on the plane.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.890
This implies that Tyler had been plotting
and acting before he ever met the Narrator

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:01.890
on the plane.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.989
"The first rule of Fight Club is: you do not
talk about Fight Club.”

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:11.400
And despite that also being the second rule
of Fight Club, the club not only continues

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.900
to attract new members, but eventually transforms
into Project Mayhem, an anarchist organization

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.110
run by Tyler.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.700
Even though Tyler scolds the club for breaking
the rule, he doesn't seem to mind that it's

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:23.700
growing.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.130
After all, breaking the rules is what Fight
Club is all about.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.260
When the Narrator runs into Bob after a long
time, Bob is excited to talk all about a new

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.080
club he's attending.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.159
"Well, the first rule is, I'm not supposed
to talk about it."

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:42.011
The Narrator tells him that he's part of the
same club too, and they talk about Fight Club

00:03:42.011 --> 00:03:43.490
without saying its name.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.380
It's possible to imagine that word of Fight
Club spread like this, with people talking

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:53.519
about the club as though it were a support
group for them which it arguably was.

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:58.380
Fight Club and Project Mayhem eventually grow
to include hundreds of members over multiple

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.580
cities, functioning as an army with plans,
weapons, and explosives.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.620
Project Mayhem aims to bomb several credit
card company buildings, an operation which

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.810
would have to take a lot of money.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:13.560
So how did Tyler and his army find the funds
needed to support such a massive undertaking?

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.880
To finance Fight Club, Tyler had been making
luxury soap from the fat dumped in the garbage

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.590
facilities of liposuction clinics, which he
would sell for $21 dollars apiece.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:28.270
Still, since Tyler seems to be the sole proprietor
of his small-scale soap business, he wouldn't

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.030
generate enough revenue alone to support the
club.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:34.100
Project Mayhem doesn't just sell soap to make
money, though.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.210
The Narrator even blackmails his boss with
compromising information about their company

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:43.009
in exchange for a full salary and perks as
an "outside consultant."

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:47.131
While this provides one source of income,
it still doesn't explain how Project Mayhem

00:04:47.131 --> 00:04:49.410
could afford to pull off their master plan.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.730
However, it is pretty likely that Project
Mayhem's many members may have contributed

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.320
their own earnings to support the club's movement.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:01.530
At the climax of the film, Tyler appears to
have the upper hand on the Narrator, since

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.400
Tyler doesn't really exist and therefore can't
be hurt.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.940
However, the Narrator eventually realizes
that if Tyler is holding a gun, so is the

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:10.940
Narrator himself.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.660
He turns the tables by taking control of Tyler's
gun himself and finding a new way to deal

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.240
with Tyler by shooting himself.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.120
The gunshot wound isn't fatal enough for the
Narrator to pass out; he can still talk to

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.830
his soldiers and Marla, although barely.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:30.090
The more you think about it, the more questions
arise about the logic of Tyler's death.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.819
What specifically causes Tyler Durden, who
exists only in the Narrator's head, to believe

00:05:34.819 --> 00:05:38.199
that he has been killed by a single bullet
to the Narrator's jaw?

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:42.780
One explanation is that the entire final showdown
is more of a mental struggle within the Narrator's

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.100
head than it is a physical one.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.800
After realizing that he is ultimately the
one in control, the Narrator shoots himself

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.690
non-fatally in order to finally get rid of
Tyler once and for all.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.370
With the tagline "Mischief.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:57.370
Mayhem.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.229
Soap." and the film's title inscribed on a
pink soap bar, soap was a key aspect of the

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:04.169
marketing and plot of the film.

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:08.919
The Narrator is introduced to Tyler Durden,
a seemingly successful luxury soap entrepreneur.

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:12.901
Only after spending more time with Tyler and
getting to know him better does the Narrator

00:06:12.901 --> 00:06:16.229
learn the gruesome way Tyler makes his soaps.

00:06:16.229 --> 00:06:21.020
While soap is a cleaning agent, the history
of soap manufacturing is unpleasant.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.860
In Ancient Mesopotamia, soap had been made
by cooking fats obtained from slaughtered

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:26.860
animals.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:31.949
Along with being symbolic of this sacrifice,
soaps also have a history of being a luxury

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:38.270
item, only being available to European aristocracy.
Tyler, who despises wealth and vanity, sells

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:44.539
soap made from the fat of rich people to the
very same people later, and no one's the wiser.

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:50.340
It fits into his anti-capitalist mantra perfectly,
which is why it's used as an icon for the

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.569
movie itself.

00:06:52.569 --> 00:06:56.800
To anyone watching "Fight Club" for the first
time, the few single-frame "blips" that appear

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.139
in the initial few minutes of the film can
be disorienting and confusing.

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:05.639
It's only after a second watch when you may
realize it was Tyler who "blipped" in these

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:09.580
frames which is something Tyler does at his
job in the film itself.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:14.360
If you pause on the frames, you would see
Tyler striking relaxed poses, as though he

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.120
were foreshadowing his meeting with the Narrator.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.349
It's also possible that Tyler had been operating
behind the Narrator's back whenever the Narrator

00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:22.610
was asleep.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:27.190
Tyler's memories and personality may have
begun to bleed into the Narrator's conscious

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:31.710
mind, which may be why Tyler finally introduces
himself to the Narrator.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.500
In Marvel's "Moon Knight," a similar situation
occurs in a more modern setting.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:39.720
Marc Spector had been able to function without
Steven Grant being aware that he has a second

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.660
personality, but eventually, it wasn't possible
to keep that a secret from Steven anymore.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:49.050
It's interesting to note that both Steven
Grant and "Fight Club's" Narrator have troubles

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:55.789
with insomnia, which play into their alternate
identities as "Moon Knight" and "Tyler Durden".

00:07:55.789 --> 00:08:00.690
Marla's frustration with the Narrator should
have been one of the first clues toward "Fight

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:04.440
Club's" big twist, that he and Tyler Durdin
are the same person.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:09.150
Upon rewatching the film, it becomes a kind
of game to find the moments and scenes where

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.430
the Narrator was actually Tyler Durden.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:17.510
The film uses his behavioral changes, intentional
continuity errors, and other easter eggs planted

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:20.320
to indicate to the audience which is which.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.979
One clear example of the Narrator being more
Tyler than himself happens when the Narrator's

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:28.460
boss confronts him on his erratic behavior
since joining Fight Club.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.199
The Narrator responds rudely and threatens
his boss.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:39.099
"He's a button-down, Oxford-cloth psycho who
might just snap and stalk from office to office…"

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:42.539
"...pumping round after round into colleagues
and coworkers."

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:48.140
This kind of intimidation seems out of character
for the usually meek Narrator, and is similar

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.550
to Brad Pitt's unsettling portrayal of Tyler
Durden.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:56.110
While Tyler's attitude and manner of life
are supposed to be influencing him, it appears

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.100
that the Narrator is almost being "possessed"
by Tyler in this scene.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.339
It's also interesting to see that the Narrator's
goatee-like stubble in the sequence matches

00:09:04.339 --> 00:09:05.610
that of Tyler's.

